# Line-ending policy for a working tree that TWO gits touch.
#
# This repo is worked on from Git for Windows (`core.autocrlf=true`) and from
# git inside WSL (`core.autocrlf` unset, i.e. false) — against the SAME files.
# With no policy the two disagree on every line: a one-line edit made from the
# "wrong" side rewrites the whole file, so diffs become unreadable and
# `git blame` loses the trail. Before this file existed, 1724 of 2369 tracked
# files were stored with CRLF.
#
# `eol=lf` (not a bare `text=auto`) is deliberate: `text=auto` alone still lets
# each git decide the WORKING-TREE ending from its own autocrlf, which is
# exactly the disagreement to remove when the working tree is shared.

# Default: normalize everything to LF in the repository AND on disk.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts break outright with CRLF — `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` is not a
# valid interpreter path. Never let a platform override these.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the mirror image: cmd.exe needs CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binaries — never touched, never diffed as text. A stray CRLF conversion here
# corrupts the file rather than reformatting it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.node binary
*.wasm binary
